  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${\u222c}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [1,e].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}(\\frac{3}{y}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A-{\u222c}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(e)-3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(e-1)[{x}^{4}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(e-1)({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =15(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A=3-15(e-1)=18-15e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
